cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "GRIP-1: Geometryx Relocation Intercomparison Protocol"
message: "If you use this protocol or submit to it, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Carson
    given-names: Justin
    affiliation: Geometryx
repository-code: "https://github.com/Carson2113/geometryx-grip"
license: MIT
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-08-23"
abstract: >-
  GRIP-1 is a public, vintage-locked evaluation protocol for forecasts of US
  metropolitan population and house-price movement, built entirely on
  public-domain federal data. It is modelled on AIMIP, the AI Model
  Intercomparison Project for machine-learning climate emulators, and adapts
  its central disciplines to housing: a ban on features that act as proxies for
  a clock, prescribed forcing via within-region and within-origin demeaning,
  expanding rolling origins rather than a single holdout, a mandatory naive
  baseline, pre-registered shock-response signs where no ground truth exists,
  and public declaration of protocol deviations.
keywords:
  - housing
  - migration
  - forecast evaluation
  - climate risk
  - insurance
  - intercomparison
  - backtesting
